  &lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-weight: 400 !important; font-size: 1.3em !important; color: #000;"&gt;&lt;a href="https://frontendfoc.us/link/181520/rss" title="lemire.me" style=" color: #D94306;"&gt;You Can Use Newline Characters (and Tabs) in URLs&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt; — Daniel clearly explains some of the nuances of the &lt;a href="https://frontendfoc.us/link/181521/rss" style=" color: #D94306;"&gt;WHATWG URL spec&lt;/a&gt;, revealing that browsers essentially allow for both newline and tab characters within URLs. Yes, it’s not actually valid, but it does work — meaning you don’t have to have unreadable long strings. Not something I was aware of!&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;Daniel Lemire &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;

